# Line endings, and the one place they are not cosmetic.
#
# `gradlew` is a POSIX shell script. Checked out with CRLF — the Git for
# Windows default, core.autocrlf=true — and copied into a Linux container, its
# shebang reads "#!/bin/sh\r"; the kernel takes the \r as part of the
# interpreter path and reports
#
#     /bin/sh: 1: ./gradlew: not found
#
# about a file that is plainly there. It happens on a Windows clone and nowhere
# else, so CI stays green while the containerised build fails on a laptop.
# Measured here: Dockerfile.build failed at exactly that line.
#
# `text eol=lf` makes these LF in the working tree on every platform, not only
# in the repository.
gradlew text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
Dockerfile.* text eol=lf

# A Windows batch file needs CRLF for the same kind of reason, in the other
# direction.
gradlew.bat text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

*.jar binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
